  #:seon.task{:name "a-prove-pass-that-changes-nothing-is-not-proposed-again"
              :plan "D4b, S5 run 2 (`docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/s5-run-2.md#Broken first`, item 1): the Design attempt's prove pass recorded one test run (`020ebb9dfc62`, `seon.step.design-test/handoff` red, 1 of 9 failing, 537 ms, 72,413 calls, 508 MB) and `seon.turn/prove-turn` then closed with `report :closed 0`, leaving the derivation unchanged, so `(seon.turn/next-agent-work head {:seon.agent/id \"attempt/9107c5cedfe0\"})` answered `{:seon.turn.work/situation :prove}` again on every pass with no bound: one run on the branch in four minutes, the turn thread hot at about one core. Root cause of this class: the `:prove` arm derives from the branch's current state, not from what changed since the agent's last answer (`:seon.agent/answered-t`), so a pass whose answer changes nothing is proposed again forever (CLAUDE.md: bound admission, derive work from a wake the agent has not answered). The recorder/reader digest mismatch that made this pass unreadable is separate (D4a, owned by lane s5-d3). Done when `seon.turn/next-agent-work` proposes `:prove` only when an input it reads (the branch's program identities, the task revision, a test result) changed after the agent's last answered t, so after a prove pass that recorded its run the answer is a model turn or nil, never `:prove` again; proof as a table row in an existing `seon.turn-test` or `seon.cluster.db-flow-test` case: a fresh `seon.task/chain!` Design attempt whose proof is red takes its prove pass once, and a second `next-agent-work` on the unchanged branch is not `:prove`. Read `seon.turn/next-agent-work`, `seon.turn/prove-turn` and `seon.turn/task-end`."}
