+ #:seon.task{:name "an-outside-agent-holding-a-task-reads-stalled"
+             :plan "`seon.turn/task-end` answers `:stalled` for an agent that works its task through MCP `eval_clj` and has made one eval request: `seon.turn/stalled?` reads its latest closed turn (the eval request's, no `:seon.turn/disposition`) as the loop having stopped calling it, and with no unanswered wake that is a stall. Measured 2026-10-15 on default (350b05df7), agent proof-pending-2 holding task `tq.pp2/only` with one red proof: `[(seon.turn/task-end (seon.db/db) \"proof-pending-2\") (seon.turn/stalled? (seon.db/db) \"proof-pending-2\")]` answered `[:stalled true]` right after its third eval request. In `seon.test-support/with-running-agent` (whose agent runs on the cluster's branch, so the turn pass records the end) the agent is retired after its first eval request: every later `eval_clj` answers `Agent ... is retired: its admission closed` (`seon.proof-reuse-test/a-red-proof-is-current-and-answered-from-its-record` calls `seon.turn/test-step` directly for this reason). Fix at `seon.turn/stalled?`: an eval request is the agent's own turn, never the loop's stop; prove with a `with-running-agent` row that holds a task, makes two eval requests and reads its admission open. Read `seon.turn/stalled?`, `seon.turn/task-end`, `seon.turn/continuing-reply?` and `seon.mcp/execution`."}]
